 11	
 12	# tasks-axi
 13	
 14	Agent ergonomic task & backlog manager for the current workspace. Tasks live in the configured backend; Markdown is the default. Prefer this over editing backend storage directly for task state, dependency, or hold changes.
 15	
 16	You do not need tasks-axi installed globally - invoke it with `npx -y tasks-axi <command>`.
 17	If tasks-axi output shows a follow-up command starting with `tasks-axi`, run it as `npx -y tasks-axi ...` instead.
 18	
 19	tasks-axi stores tasks in the configured backend. Markdown is the default and uses a hand-editable `backlog.md` with a byte-exact round-trip. Archive-body, prune, render, and CLI move are Markdown-only and unavailable with ADO.
 20	
 21	## When to use
 22	
 23	Use tasks-axi whenever a task touches the backlog: filing or dispatching work, moving a task through queued -> in flight -> done, recording a PR url or report path on completion, tracking blocked-by dependencies, pausing dispatch with structured holds, finding dispatchable ready work or intentionally held work, or trimming the Done list.
 24	
 25	## Workflow
 26	
 27	1. Run `npx -y tasks-axi` with no arguments for a dashboard of the current backlog - in flight work, queued work with blockers, and suggested next commands.
 28	2. Drill in verb-first: `list`, `show <id>`, `ready`, then mutate with `add`, `start`, `done`, `block`/`unblock`, `hold`/`unhold`, `update`.
 29	3. The long notes never appear in `list`; run `show <id> --full` to read a task's complete body before replacing it.
 30	4. `add` takes a caller-supplied id (the join key), e.g. `tasks-axi add fm-x "title" --kind ship --repo firstmate --start`; or pass `--mint` to generate a slug-xx id from the title.
 31	5. `done <id> --pr <url>` (or `--report <path>`) closes a task and records the link. With Markdown, it also prunes the Done list (archived, never deleted). Then `ready` shows work it unblocked.
 32	6. `hold <id> --reason "<text>"` pauses dispatch without prose parsing; `ready` excludes active holds by default, and `ready --include-held` shows a separate held group.
 33	   Use `--until YYYY-MM-DD` for a date gate that becomes inactive on and after that date.
 34	7. Human-readable responses include contextual next-step hints under `help:` when there is a useful follow-up - follow them.
 35	8. `--json` mutation responses skip `help:` and return the deterministic result object instead.
 36	
 37	## Commands
 38	
 39	```
 40	commands[19]:
 41	  (none)=dashboard, add, list, show, start, done, reopen, update, rm, block, unblock, hold, unhold, ready, public-followup, mv, prune, render, setup
 42	```
 43	
 44	Run `npx -y tasks-axi --help` for global flags, or `npx -y tasks-axi <command> --help` for per-command usage.
 45	
 46	## Tips
 47	
 48	- Output is TOON-encoded and token-efficient; the long task body is truncated by default - the whole point is that `list` stays cheap.
 49	  Use `--full` only when you need the complete notes.
 50	- Every write leads with an `ok:` line confirming the write result, including the resulting task state when the command changes one (e.g. `ok: start <id> -> In flight`, `ok: done <id> -> Done (pr <url>)`, `ok: render -> normalized <n>`), then state-aware next-step hints.
 51	  Mutations are idempotent and add `already: true` on a no-op; re-running is safe.
 52	- Pass `--json` to any mutation supported by the active backend for a machine-readable result object (`{ "ok": true, "action": ..., "task": { ... } }` or operation-specific result fields) instead of TOON - confirm a write deterministically without a follow-up read.
 53	- `block <id> --by <other>` and `unblock` manage the dependency graph; `hold <id> --reason "<text>" [--until YYYY-MM-DD]` and `unhold` manage structured dispatch pauses; `ready` lists only queued work with no unresolved blocker and no active hold.
 54	- Filter `list` with `--state`, `--repo`, `--kind`, `--blocked`, `--limit`, and add columns with `--fields a,b,c`.
 55	  Use `list --state held` or `--fields held,hold_reason,hold_kind,hold_until` when scanning active hold state.
 56	- Existing prose markers such as `HELD`, `PARKED`, `DEFERRED`, `CAPTAIN-DECISION`, and `do not dispatch` stay prose until intentionally migrated.
 57	  Preserve the original prose as the hold reason, then choose `captain`, `parked`, `future`, `load`, or `external` only when the text supports that bucket.
 58	- Note writes are inspect-then-update: run `show <id> --full`, then replace the curated current body with `update <id> --body "<text>"` or `--body-file <path>`.
 59	  With Markdown, add `--archive-body` to preserve the superseded body in `note-archive.md`; `--title "<text>"` replaces the title; `render` normalizes the file; `mv <id> [<id>...] --to <path>` moves one or more tasks to another backlog in one atomic transaction - pass a whole connected set (a blocker and its dependents) to move it together and preserve its `blocked-by` links and reason strings; moves that would strand an endpoint are refused.
 60	- Free-form (no-id) backlog lines are preserved verbatim and are never modified.
